.TH SSOOSSH 1 "2026-08-16" "ssoossh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ssoossh \- turn an OIDC login into a short-lived SSH certificate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssoossh
.RI [ --config
.IR file ]
.RI [ --server
.IR url ]
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ args ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssoossh
is the client for an ssoosshd server. Invoked from
.IR ssh_config (5)
as a
.B ProxyCommand
or
.B "Match exec"
target, it generates a fresh keypair, hands the public key to the ssoosshd
server, opens a browser for OIDC authentication, and loads the signed
certificate into a running
.BR ssh-agent (1)
\(em or writes key and certificate files when no agent is available. Private
keys never leave the machine.
.PP
A certificate that is already loaded and still has meaningful validity left
is reused rather than replaced, so one browser approval can cover a whole
working session instead of every connection.
.PP
Runs on Linux, macOS, and Windows. Besides interactive login, the command
tree also has (currently unimplemented, see
.B COMMANDS
below) placeholders for host certificate enrollment/renewal, per-host
principal mapping for
.BR sshd_config (5)'s
.BR AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand ,
and service-account certificates for unattended jobs.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These flags are accepted before or after the command name and apply to every
subcommand.
.TP
.BI "-c, --config " file
Path to the ssoossh client config file to use instead of the normal search
path. See
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5).
.TP
.BI "--server " url
Overrides the configured server address for this invocation. Include the
scheme (e.g.
.IR https://ssh.example.com );
https is assumed if the scheme is omitted.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "ssoossh ssh login"
Authenticate via OIDC and load a signed SSH certificate into the agent (or
key files). Generates a fresh keypair, sends the public key to the server,
opens the browser for OIDC approval, and waits over server-sent events for
the signed certificate. This is what
.B "Match exec"
runs before a connection.
.PP
A certificate that is already loaded and still valid is reused; the request
that would otherwise be made is skipped and the command exits 0 immediately.
.TP
.B --force
Request a new certificate even if a valid one is already loaded. Replaces
the certificate this login supersedes in the agent (or on disk) after the
new one is loaded, subject to best-effort removal.
.TP
.BI "--key-type " type
SSH key algorithm to generate for this login only:
.IR ed25519 ", " ecdsa ", " or " rsa ".
Overrides
.B sshkey.type
in the config file for this invocation. Default depends on FIPS mode; see
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5).
.TP
.BI "--key-size " bits
Key size for this login only: bits for rsa, curve for ecdsa (256, 384, or
521), ignored for ed25519. Overrides
.B sshkey.size
in the config file for this invocation.
.PP
Everything this command prints for a human \(em the approval URL, wait
status, and result \(em goes to stderr; stdout is left free because
.B "Match exec"
and interactive use both treat this command's exit status, not its stdout,
as the result.
.SS "ssoossh ssh logout"
Removes the certificates ssoossh put in the agent \(em those signed by the
configured CA \(em and nothing else; the user's own keys and certificates
from other CAs are left alone. When key files are used instead of an agent,
the files ssoossh wrote are deleted.
.SS "ssoossh ssh proxycommand \fIcommand\fR [args...]"
For use as
.IR ssh_config (5)'s
.BR ProxyCommand .
Ensures a valid certificate is loaded (equivalent to
.B "ssh login"
without
.BR --force ),
then
.BR exec (2)s
.I command
with
.I args
exactly as configured, relaying stdio to it. Requires a running SSH agent:
key files are not supported here, because
.BR ssh (1)
reads key files once at startup and never sees a certificate written after
that.
.PP
Everything after
.B proxycommand
on the command line is treated as the command to exec, not as flags of
.BR ssoossh 's
own.
.PP
Example, adapting an
.IR ssh_config (5)
.B ProxyCommand
line:
.RS
.nf
Before: ProxyCommand /usr/bin/nc -X connect -x 192.0.2.0:8080 %h %p
After : ProxyCommand ssoossh ssh proxycommand /usr/bin/nc -X connect -x 192.0.2.0:8080 %h %p
.fi
.RE
.SS "ssoossh ssh inspect"
Prints the certificates ssoossh currently holds in the agent (or key files):
principals, key ID, type, expiry, serial number, extensions, and critical
options, in a layout deliberately close to
.BR "ssh-keygen -L" .
Writes to stdout.
.SS "ssoossh ssh config"
Prints the effective client configuration as actually resolved \(em after
merging every config file, applying defaults, and deciding the key
algorithm \(em rather than the contents of any one file. Loading the
configuration is itself the validation: an unusable combination is reported
as an error before this prints anything. Writes to stdout.
.SS "ssoossh host sign, ssoossh host renew, ssoossh host sync, ssoossh host principals"
Host certificate management. These subcommands exist in the command tree
with their documented names, descriptions, and
.B --help
text, but every one of them currently returns a
.RB \(dq "not implemented" \(dq
error when run \(em none has an implementation yet. The intended design, for
reference:
.RS
.TP
.B sign
First-time issuance of a host certificate, vouched for by a human through
the OIDC approval chain (the anti-MITM control for host certificates).
.TP
.B renew
Subsequent issuance once a valid host certificate already exists,
authenticated by that certificate rather than a fresh OIDC approval.
.TP
.B sync
Pull the principal mapping from the server and write it locally, for
.B "host principals"
to answer from.
.TP
.B principals
Implements
.BR sshd_config (5)'s
.BR AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand :
intended to run as root, be called on every login attempt, and never touch
the network \(em answering only from whatever
.B "host sync"
last wrote locally.
.RE
.SS "ssoossh service enroll, ssoossh service retrieve"
Service-account (non-interactive) certificate enrollment. Also present in
the command tree but not yet implemented; each currently returns a
.RB \(dq "not implemented" \(dq
error. Intended design:
.RS
.TP
.B enroll
Enroll a service keypair (operator-supplied, or client-generated) for
unattended certificate issuance. After OIDC approval the server is meant to
return an enrollment code bound to both the public key and the authorized
option set.
.TP
.B retrieve
Retrieve a service certificate using a previously issued enrollment code,
posting only the code \(em never resubmitting the public key.
.RE
.SS "ssoossh ca"
Retrieves and prints the CA public key of the configured server, for use in
.BR sshd_config (5)'s
.B TrustedUserCAKeys
or an
.B @cert-authority
line in
.BR known_hosts (5).
Writes to stdout.
.SS "ssoossh version"
Prints the client's version, commit, build date, and builder. Works even
when the configuration failed to load or the server is unreachable, since
it does none of the normal startup work the other commands do.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Non-zero on any failure, including configuration errors, a
denied or expired certificate request, and (for the unimplemented
.B host
and
.B service
subcommands) always, since they return an error unconditionally.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ssoossh/ssoossh.yaml
System-wide configuration; also the only location the
.B enforce
setting is read from. See
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/ssoossh.yaml
Per-user configuration (roaming
.IR %AppData%\essoossh\essoossh.yaml
on Windows).
.TP
.I ./ssoossh.yaml
Working-directory configuration, for development.
.SH EXAMPLES
Typical
.IR ssh_config (5)
setup, matching a host pattern and running
.B "ssh login"
before letting the normal SSH connection proceed:
.RS
.nf
Match host *.internal.example.com exec "ssoossh ssh login"
    User myusername
.fi
.RE
Or as a ProxyCommand that also handles the relay:
.RS
.nf
Host *.internal.example.com
    ProxyCommand ssoossh ssh proxycommand /usr/bin/nc %h %p
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5),
.BR ssoosshd (8),
.BR pam_ssoossh (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR ssh-agent (1)
